GAMERGIRL — MULTI-WORLD RUNNING GUIDE (STAGE 5.2)
================================================

The IDE now generates gamehelpers/gameworlds.c/.h from ALL published
worlds/<name>/ folders.  It works with ONE world too: start(NULL).
Each world has unique code and object instances. Only the active world's
assets/maps/atlas and object state should be initialised at runtime.

IMPORTANT: YOUR main.c IS NEVER REPLACED AUTOMATICALLY.

1. Regenerate EACH of your .sbgm designs using Update Game Project once,
   so all worlds have the Stage 5.2 public APIs and inventory hooks.
2. Include "gamehelpers/gameworlds.h" in main.c.
3. Replace canonical gameassets_init / world1_load_map / load_atlas / 
   gameadventure_setup / gameworlditems_init calls with:

       if (!gameworlds_start("world1")) { /* handle load failure */ }

   Or use gameworlds_start(NULL) to choose the last generated world.
4. Use gameworlds_map() when rebinding player physics to the new world.
   A mapless world returns NULL: handle scenery-only worlds explicitly.
5. Dispatch object animation and drawing via gameworlds_update(),
   gameworlds_draw() and gameworlds_draw_behind(). Foreground/background
   drawing uses gameworlds_draw_foreground / gameworlds_draw_background.
6. For a door/collision/trigger, queue a change from inside the callback:

       gameworlds_request("forest");

   At a SAFE FRAME BOUNDARY after finishing collision/drawing:

       if (!gameworlds_apply_pending()) { /* handle failed load */ }

   Do NOT do an immediate unload inside the collision or drawing code.
7. Save and restore the player using gameworlds_player_hooks(). Your
   save hook copies score/HP/velocity etc into gameworlds_player_state_t;
   your restore hook creates/rebinds the player for the NEW map, then
   places them at a suitable spawn point. The FIRST gameworlds_start()
   does not restore an uninitialised player: initialise the first player
   normally once the first world has loaded. See the example below.
8. Inventory items with the same Adventure name across worlds share
   quantities. Missing item names keep their counts until they appear
   in another world. Worlds without Adventure are supported.
9. When main.c has FULLY migrated off canonical gameassets/gameworlditems/
   gameadventure/splinepaths modules, add this exact comment to main.c:

       /* GAMERGIRL_WORLD_MANAGER_MAIN */

   This opts your project into excluding those OLD compiled copies.
   Do NOT add the marker while main.c still refers to legacy symbols!

--- PLAYER CALLBACK EXAMPLE ---

/* In your own main.c, after declaring player_physics and score/health: */
static void save_player(gameworlds_player_state_t *s, void *ctx)
{
    (void)ctx;
    s->score = score;
    s->health = player_health;
    s->player_x100 = player_physics.x100;
    s->player_y100 = player_physics.y100;
    s->player_vy100 = player_physics.vy100;
}
static void restore_player(gameworlds_player_state_t *s, void *ctx)
{
    (void)ctx;
    score = s->score;
    player_health = s->health;
    /* IMPORTANT: rebind physics to a sprite owned by the NEW world and
       the NEW map before reusing saved coordinates or calling physics.
       For a different spawn point, use game_tile_player_reset(). */
    /* game_tile_player_start(&player_physics, gameworlds_map(), NEW_PLAYER_SPRITE); */
    player_physics.x100 = s->player_x100;
    player_physics.y100 = s->player_y100;
    player_physics.vy100 = s->player_vy100;
}

/* Before gameworlds_start(): */
gameworlds_player_hooks(save_player, restore_player, NULL);

A gfxbob_t contains a pointer to world-owned sprite pixels. NEVER retain
or reuse an old world's player BOB once its assets have been unloaded.
For a game that needs a player in every world, define that player sprite in
each world or implement a separate permanent/shared player asset. This stage
does NOT automatically invent a missing player or create a door/spawn editor.

The state is retained across world transitions IN RAM, not saved to TFCard.
State per-world such as collected chests and defeated enemies is reset when
that world is reloaded; persistent world completion flags are your game's
responsibility. Runtime loading/asset safety works without a GUI trigger.

For single-world projects simply call gameworlds_start(NULL) once, and
use gameworlds_shutdown() at exit. During migration old main.c continues to
compile, but it still runs through the legacy root-level resources until
YOU change it to use the new gameworlds API.

STAGE 5.48: DOOR-SELECTED TRANSITIONS AND LOCATION CIRCLES
--------------------------------------------------------
Each saved default/named Player Start configures the pair of effects used by
Goto World doors THAT TARGET THAT START: OUT plays over the departing world,
and IN plays after the destination world and player have loaded. Both may be
None (instant), Fade, Swipe Left/Right/Up/Down or Circle. Duration is 1-150
frames. At startup only the initial world's default IN mode is used.

Circle centre defaults to Screen centre (backwards compatible). Set it to
"Door / Player Start" in the destination Player Start editor if Circle Close
should centre on the exiting Goto World object and Circle Open should centre
on that arrival Player Start. Screen positioning follows the actual camera
scroll, not raw world pixels. Handwritten gameworlds_request_at() calls have
no doorway position; in that case the outgoing effect uses the last active
Player Start instead. For an explicit outgoing position, call
 gameworlds_request_at_from(world, start_id, exit_world_x, exit_world_y).

IDE-owned main.c contains all integration. For user-owned main.c, use the
regenerated maingame.c.txt and include these FOUR integration points:
  1. After player/buffer setup: gameworlds_transitions_enable(1);
  2. Gate physics/input/updates with if (!gameworlds_transition_busy()).
  3. After all drawing, before gfx_dispfbuffer, while camera_x/y are current:
         gameworlds_transition_camera(camera_x, camera_y);
  4. Immediately after that: gameworlds_transition_frame(VIEW_W, VIEW_H);

Old user-owned main.c continues to compile. No opt-in means instant cuts;
omitting just the camera call means location circles fall back to centre.
Fade uses temporary front/back CLUT copies (original PPB pixel indices are
unchanged). Spatial effects use foreground index 255 temporarily as black;
reserve that index if you want clean wipes. Regenerate ALL world designs and
the project-level registry when upgrading. No extra PPB files are created.

STAGE 5.154: LIGHTNING-AWARE SPOTLIGHT / WEATHER STATUS
------------------------------------------------------
Within each WORLD WEATHER panel, "Suspend spotlight during lightning flashes"
skips spotlight rendering on only the bright lightning-pulse frames. This is
off by default for backward compatibility; enabling it needs no main.c edits.
The weather renderer still runs after spotlight and before the HUD.

For custom effects, the generated gameworlds.h supplies the named bit flags
GAME_WEATHER_STATUS_PRECIPITATION and GAME_WEATHER_STATUS_LIGHTNING_FLASH,
with gameworlds_weather_status() safe to query before the spotlight draw call.
gameworlds_weather_frame() now returns the same bit mask without changing its
rendering effect, so old code that ignores its return value still works.
See Stage 5.154 README.md for the exact optional main.c code snippet.

STAGE 5.155: OPTIONAL LIGHTNING SCREEN OVERLAY
---------------------------------------------
WORLD WEATHER now includes "Enable lightning screen overlay" (checked by
legacy default). You can untick it to retain only the optional FRONT/BACK
palette flash. "Overlay flash strength" and "Palette flash strength" are
independent and share the same double-flash timing. Palette-only pulses still
report GAME_WEATHER_STATUS_LIGHTNING_FLASH, so optional spotlight suspension
continues to work. If both effects are off, the status flash bit is clear.
The original .pal files are untouched. Real palette flashes can also tint
HUD/dialogue colours because they share the indexed display colour tables.
Re-export the world/registry when upgrading; main.c requires no new calls.

STAGE 5.156: INDEPENDENT GAMEPLAY / PARTICLE / WEATHER CLOCKS
-------------------------------------------------------------
Your hand-maintained main.c can PAUSE only gameplay during a conversation:

    /* Outside the game_adventure_open() / dialogue guard, once per 50-Hz frame: */
    game_update_weather(1);
    game_update_particles(1);

    /* In the existing gameplay guard, replacing gameworlds_update(1): */
    if (!game_adventure_open()) {
        gameworlds_update_gameplay(1);
        /* your existing player physics, enemies, collision, etc. */
    }

The long API names gameworlds_update_weather/particles/gameplay are
identical to the short helpers. Pick ONE spelling; don't call both.
The old gameworlds_update(1) remains supported and still updates all three,
so DON'T call it in a frame where you use the split calls (double update).
For world-transition safety, keep the two ambience update calls under your
existing !gameworlds_transition_busy() outer guard.

IMPORTANT: Regenerate ALL published worlds and then Update Game Project so
new world registry function pointers are present. Older published worlds
still load, but their legacy object update cannot independently animate
particles while the game is paused until that world is re-exported.

Stage 5.156 maingame.c.txt already demonstrates the proper placement. The
IDE never overwrites your authored main.c. No changes to the weather and
particle rendering calls, pre-population, or drawing order are required.
